            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Equate positions, check the solution, then differentiate each displacement and take the magnitude.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Equate the displacements. Put $u=\\sqrt t$, where $u\\geq0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt t&=\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt t}\\\\t&=2+\\sqrt t\\\\u^2-u-2&=0\\\\(u-2)(u+1)&=0\\\\u&=2\\\\t&=4.\\end{align*}$$\nChecking in the original expressions gives $x_A(4)=x_B(4)=2$, so the particles collide there. Both velocities at $t=4$ are positive, so their speeds equal their velocities.\n$$\\begin{align*}v_A(t)&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt t}\\\\v_A(4)&=\\frac14\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}\\\\v_B(t)&=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt t\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt t}}\\\\v_B(4)&=\\frac1{16}\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
